---
import './about.scss'
import Breadcrumbs from '@/components/breadcrumbs/Breadcrumbs.astro'
import { SITE } from '@/const/site'
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro'

const details = [
	['Location', 'Ukraine'],
	['Work', 'Software engineering'],
	['Interests', 'Writing, books, design, games, training'],
	['Languages', 'Ukrainian, English'],
] as const
---

<Layout
	title={`About - ${SITE.name}`}
	description="About andrii stavskyi, a Full-Stack Software Engineer from Ukraine."
>
	<div class="page-stack">
		<Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About' }]} />

		<section class="section" aria-labelledby="about-title">
			<header>
				<h1 id="about-title">About</h1>
			</header>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					I'm a Full-Stack Software Engineer from Ukraine working primarily with
					.NET and modern web technologies.
				</p>
				<p>
					I prefer software that is predictable, restrained, and easy to reason
					about. The same preference carries into interface design: clear
					structure, little decoration, and interactions that do not ask for
					attention unless they need it.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<hr />

		<section class="section" aria-labelledby="work-title">
			<header><h2 id="work-title">Work</h2></header>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					I care more about software remaining understandable six months later
					than about making it look clever today.
				</p>
				<p>
					Most of my attention goes to structure, naming, predictable behavior,
					and keeping responsibilities clear. I like systems where a change has
					an obvious place to belong and where the code explains its own shape
					without relying on unnecessary abstraction.
				</p>
				<p>
					I work across backend and frontend development, but I tend to think
					about both in the same way: reduce ambiguity, keep boundaries clear,
					and avoid complexity that has not earned its place.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<hr />

		<section class="section" aria-labelledby="side-projects-title">
			<header><h2 id="side-projects-title">Side projects</h2></header>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					I usually start personal projects for one of two reasons: there is a
					small problem I want solved properly, or there is an idea I want to
					understand by building it.
				</p>
				<p>
					I prefer small applications with a clear purpose over large projects
					built only to demonstrate a stack. Soliloquy is one example of that
					approach: local-first, narrow in scope, and designed around a specific
					way of writing rather than a long feature list.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<hr />

		<section class="section" aria-labelledby="writing-title">
			<header><h2 id="writing-title">Writing and books</h2></header>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>I spend a lot of time reading and writing outside software.</p>
				<p>
					I am particularly drawn to stories built around discipline, consequence,
					identity, endurance, and the cost of becoming stronger. Berserk,
					Vagabond, and Vinland Saga are recurring references for me because they
					treat growth as something difficult, uneven, and rarely clean.
				</p>
				<p>
					I am also interested in dark fantasy more broadly, especially worlds
					that feel old, hostile, and only partially explained.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<hr />

		<section class="section" aria-labelledby="games-title">
			<header><h2 id="games-title">Games</h2></header>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					The Dark Souls series has had a lasting influence on the way I think
					about games and, to some extent, design in general.
				</p>
				<p>
					I like systems that do not over-explain themselves, worlds that reward
					attention, and mechanics that become clear through use rather than
					instruction. Restraint is part of what makes those games work: they
					leave enough unsaid for the player to participate in understanding
					them.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<hr />

		<section class="section" aria-labelledby="design-title">
			<header><h2 id="design-title">Design</h2></header>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>
					I prefer interfaces that explain themselves through structure rather
					than animation or decoration.
				</p>
				<p>
					Good design, to me, is often subtractive. Fewer competing elements,
					fewer states that need explanation, fewer interactions that exist only
					because they can.
				</p>
				<p>
					I am especially interested in older web design, where limited tools
					often forced stronger hierarchy and more direct communication. I do not
					think old interfaces were better by default, but I think some of their
					restraint is worth keeping.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<hr />

		<section class="section" aria-labelledby="training-title">
			<header><h2 id="training-title">Training</h2></header>
			<div class="prose">
				<p>I train regularly and value the routine more than the novelty.</p>
				<p>
					Progress there is usually repetitive and unremarkable: show up, do the
					work, recover, repeat. I like that. It is a useful counterweight to work
					that is often abstract and difficult to measure day to day.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		<hr />

		<section class="section" aria-labelledby="details-title">
			<header><h2 id="details-title">Details</h2></header>
			<table class="details-table">
				<tbody>
					{details.map(([label, value]) => (
						<tr>
							<th scope="row">{label}</th>
							<td>{value}</td>
						</tr>
					))}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</section>
	</div>
</Layout>
